{
  "image_name": "5c1eda259ecc7b192fc4bd172da3f7c0.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how a tensor-network style computation can be implemented as a sequence of quantum circuits with repeated measurements and data input. A simple vertex or tensor node is first shown as equivalent to a unitary block acting on an incoming data line together with an auxiliary qubit. The auxiliary qubit is prepared or measured, and one output is projected onto the \\(|0\\rangle\\) state, leaving the transformed data on the remaining line. A second schematic extends this idea to a node with two message-like outputs and an additional injected input \\(d\\), again represented by a unitary plus a measured ancilla.\n\nThe lower part of the figure shows the main idea at scale: a chain of connected nodes can be unfolded into a time-ordered quantum circuit. Instead of many separate graph nodes, the same kind of unitary block is applied repeatedly along a horizontal wire carrying intermediate states labeled \\(m_1, m_2, m_3,\\) and \\(m_4\\). At each step, a fresh data input \\(d\\) enters from below, an ancilla qubit \\(q\\) is measured, and one output is postselected or reset to \\(|0\\rangle\\). Overall, the figure conveys a mapping from a graphical network model to sequential circuit execution over time.",
